const TopicLink = ({ id, children }: { id: string; children: React.ReactNode }) => <a href={`#help/${id}`} data-help-topic={id}>{children}</a>;

export function helpTopics(): HelpTopic[] {
  return [
    { id: "part-limits", title: "Part limits, stress and headroom reports", keywords: "rating voltage current thermal temperature power mechanical stress frequency pass fail incomplete margin derating headroom allowance", body: <><p>Open <b>Engineering review → Part limits &amp; stress</b>. Choose a board occurrence and part, label the pin/case, then enter lower/upper limits and an explicit percentage reference. Temperature uses an allowed rise from a reference such as ambient.</p><Steps><li>Record the rating source and operating conditions.</li><li>Enter a captured observed value and result/measurement ID, revision, case, units and peak/RMS/time coverage. Mark it reviewed after checking it.</li><li>Save the rule, then Run review. Limits are inclusive; stress above 100% and negative headroom show exceedances. Missing evidence or unchecked parts stay unknown/incomplete.</li><li>Inspect the per-part summary and export complete JSON or CSV. Save with results retains observations; result-free export keeps rules and clears observations and review assertions.</li></Steps><p>These are declared allowance comparisons, not manufacturer qualification, reliability or a combined multiphysics stress law. Observations are not automatically inferred from arbitrary solver fields.</p><TopicLink id="doc:docs/PART_LIMITS.md">Equations, provenance, examples and limits</TopicLink></> },
    { id: "field-volume", title: "Voxels, streamlines and saved playback", keywords: "volume voxel CFD CHT velocity arrows streamlines streamtubes strings connected curves temperature transient frame scrub loop playback Blender photoreal", body: <>
      <p>Search <b>Volume and airflow visualizer</b> with Ctrl+K to inspect a local exported field without running a solver. Admitted native cavity and supported assembly airflow results can send fields to the same main viewport.</p>
      <Steps><li>Use the compact Fields icon in the existing viewport ribbon, then import a <code>spike/field-volume-view/v1</code> JSON file, or choose Show fields in main viewport from a CFD result. Board geometry and field layers share one scene, camera and navigation controls. Hover or focus expands the icon; click opens contextual settings.</li><li>Select the quantity and Voxels or Points. Voxels require supplied cell dimensions. Native cavity Z thickness is display-only; assembly samples without cell sizes stay points.</li><li>Toggle Velocity arrows, adjust opacity and display budget, then reveal cells with an X/Y/Z cut. Arrows are velocity glyphs, not streamlines.</li><li>For saved histories use first/previous/next/last, scrub, play/pause, speed and loop. Uneven saved timestamps are respected, with no interpolated solved fields. Static data cannot become a transient simulation.</li><li>Export field data to retain the admitted cells and frames. The viewer does not automatically save imported datasets into the active project.</li></Steps>
      <p>Temperature can display in Celsius while retaining Kelvin source data. Colors and arrow lengths use fixed ranges across saved frames. Display thinning never changes the source mesh or solver values. Playback controls review results; they do not control solver execution.</p>
      <h2>Connected flow curves</h2><p>Choose <b>Streamlines</b> for colored connected lines or <b>Streamtubes</b> for thicker tubes. Supplied curves retain their individual points and scalar colors. Adjust Stream width and opacity. Separate curves are never joined.</p><p>A declared complete 3D fluid grid can also generate approximate frozen-field streamtubes in the shared Three scene. Choose a center seed plane and density. These tubes are colored by speed; their radius is cosmetic. Sparse samples, grids with unknown gaps/solids and 2D sheets cannot safely supply this mode. Imported curve/grid data must meet the documented format. Frozen-time flow curves are not transient particle paths, and renderer-generated paths are not solved fields.</p>
      <p><b>Blender integration is planned.</b> The staged design covers static geometry/field bundles, timestamped animation and optional sparse volumes; no Blender render adapter is currently delivered.</p><p><TopicLink id="doc:docs/FIELD_VISUALIZATION.md">Field format, limits and workflow</TopicLink> · <TopicLink id="doc:docs/BLENDER_RENDER_PLAN.md">Blender integration plan</TopicLink></p>
    </> },
    { id: "ui-navigation", title: "UI map and navigation", keywords: "UX UI flow menu depth hierarchy sections organization navigate two rows ribbon tabs panels icons search restore hidden workspace", body: <>
      <p><b>Clean has two persistent command rows.</b> The global header manages the project, search and workspace. The second row shares document/assembly tabs with tiny viewport controls. Extra options open as contextual menus or focused panels.</p>
      <Figure src="/help/ui-current/navigation-map.svg" caption="Authored map of the current development Clean layout, command paths and study loop. This explains the interface; it is not a screenshot or solver result." />
      <h2>Choose your route</h2><ul><li><b>Task first:</b> Ctrl+K → action, part, net, solver or supported sentence. Review the target and complete missing engineering inputs.</li><li><b>Domain first:</b> workspace icon → Home, Mesh, Solve, PI, HF / SI, EM, Thermal, Probes, Results, Reports, Extensions or Settings. Then open the relevant contextual tool.</li><li><b>Object first:</b> selection filter → pick geometry → inspect identity/properties → contextual action.</li></ul>
      <h2>Sections and depth</h2><p>Menu groups commands into <b>Project</b>, <b>Design &amp; view</b>, <b>Analyze</b>, <b>Results &amp; reports</b>, <b>Tools</b> and <b>Help</b>. Count choices after opening Menu, including the final action: <b>Project → New project</b> is two; <b>Analyze → Power integrity → DC PI setup</b> is three; <b>Design &amp; view → Viewport tools → Navigate → Orientation grid</b> is four. Some workspace tool-group routes take five choices. Two persistent rows do not impose a two-click limit.</p><p>Use Menu search or <b>All actions</b> to find leaf actions with their full paths. <b>Back</b> or Escape moves up one Menu level; Escape at the root closes it. Icon popovers expose camera presets, selection filters and view choices directly. Disabled commands still require their prerequisites.</p>
      <h2>Tabs, panels and recovery</h2><p>A workspace chooses the workflow; an editor tab chooses the document. Assembly-view tabs choose the main scene or an occurrence-scoped view. Inspectors and setup tools open as panels with their own header controls. Use hide/restore, scale, resize, expand and supported native detach actions. <b>Show hidden tools</b> or search restores a hidden tool. Switching tabs and hiding panels preserve supported drafts; explicitly save the project before closing or reloading.</p>
      <h2>The study loop</h2><p><b>Import → review → setup → mesh/preflight → solve → inspect results → save/report.</b> Resolve missing inputs and warnings in setup. Solver availability, a prepared case and a validated result are different states.</p><p><TopicLink id="doc:docs/UI_NAVIGATION.md">Full section map, route table and navigation guide</TopicLink> · <TopicLink id="focus-workspace">Clean / Classic and appearance</TopicLink> · <TopicLink id="analytics-overview">Analysis workflows</TopicLink></p>
    </> },
    { id: "focus-workspace", title: "Clean and Classic workspace", keywords: "clean classic menu ribbon quick tools graphite light amoled hidden tools command search document tabs camera filter", body: <><p><b>Clean</b> is the default interface. It keeps the viewport prominent and keeps the legacy command ribbon unavailable; the separate optional quick-tools bar starts hidden. Use <b>Menu</b> for Project, Design &amp; view, Analyze, Results &amp; reports, Tools and Help. Use <b>Workspaces: …</b> to change workspaces. <b>Classic</b> restores the menu bar, persistent workspace tabs and expanded ribbon; both layouts use the same project and solver inputs.</p><p>Use <b>Search commands, parts, nets…</b> or Ctrl+K for the global search. The Menu panel also has <b>Search commands…</b>; it searches command labels and category paths, while <b>All actions</b> lists every action. Back or Escape returns one Menu level.</p><p>The small controls above the viewport are contextual. <b>View</b> opens camera, appearance and selection commands; the optional quick-tools bar adds actions for the current workspace, selection and result. Enable it with <b>Show optional quick-tools bar</b> in <b>Settings → Interface</b> or global search. The selection filter remains <b>All</b>, <b>Parts</b> or <b>Nets</b>.</p><p>Editors open as document tabs beside <b>Board &amp; results</b>; switching tabs retains drafts. Assembly views use <b>Main assembly</b> and <b>+ Add view</b>, and can be detached. Hide or minimize a tool to recover viewport space. Use the eye-shaped <b>Show hidden tools</b> control, <b>Tools → Open panels</b>, or <b>Tools → Windows &amp; tools</b> to restore it.</p><p><b>Settings → Interface</b> controls the workspace theme independently from the viewport. Workspace choices are <b>Graphite + Teal</b>, <b>Light + Teal</b>, <b>High contrast</b> and <b>Follow system</b>; viewport choices are <b>Graphite</b>, <b>Light</b> and <b>AMOLED · true black</b>. Changing either does not change result values.</p></> },
    { id: "assembly-placement", title: "Move assembly boards precisely", keywords: "multi board translation rotate angle arrows rings numeric delta mm degrees native window monitor", body: <><p>Open <b>Multi-board workspace</b> from global search, <b>Menu → Project</b>, or the <b>Multi-board</b> command in the Home ribbon when that ribbon is expanded. In the workspace use <b>Placement settings</b>, <b>Layers</b>, <b>Nets</b> and <b>Connector links</b>. Detached tools can be restored with <b>Show hidden tools</b>.</p><Steps><li>Select a board and choose Move or Rotate. The gizmo uses board-local axes.</li><li>Click an arrow or rotation ring, or begin dragging. Type a signed delta: 12 means 12 mm for translation or 12 degrees for rotation.</li><li>Enter applies the preview; Escape restores the starting placement. A plain drag applies on release.</li><li>Use Placement settings for absolute assembly coordinates, Euler angles, alignment snap and presentation explosion. Finish unsaved structural drafts before moving boards from another tool.</li></Steps><p>Model warnings remain available in Notifications and the assembly workspace. Physical placement changes invalidate old geometry-bound results.</p></> },
    { id: "coupled-multiboard", title: "Coupled multi-board studies", keywords: "PI SI thermal EM mutual inductance connector contact board stack harness shared solve results", body: <><p>Open <b>Multi-board workspace</b>, retain at least two board occurrences, then review <b>Connector links</b> and the coupled study editor. The earlier <b>Home → Multi-board</b> path only applies when the optional ribbon is visible.</p><Steps><li>Choose PI, SI, Thermal or EM. Enter the reduced board models and physical connector/contact properties. Blank fields are required model inputs; zero explicitly selects an ideal property.</li><li>PI/SI share one circuit with board-local connector:pin terminals, sources, loads and explicit returns. Thermal shares a network through defined contacts. EM uses supplied closed-loop magnetic models.</li><li>Run, select a result frequency where applicable, and review occurrence results, raw conservation residuals, convergence status and limitations. Contact losses from DC PI can be assigned manually to reviewed thermal nodes.</li><li>Save a setup, save with results, or export a standalone study/results file. A changed placement, graph or setup makes retained geometry-bound results stale.</li></Steps><p>These are reduced experimental or approximate models. Imported artwork does not automatically become complete board circuits, airflow domains or coupled fields. Use <TopicLink id="analytics-multiboard">Multi-board analytics</TopicLink> for the retained-volume electrothermal route and its separate limits.</p></> },
    { id: "dock-panels", title: "Expand and hide dock panels", keywords: "dock panel title collapse expand minimize restore navigator inspector bottom console probe table power tree PI setup board nets window", body: <><p>Click <b>Scene Navigator</b> or the right setup/inspector title to collapse its side panel. Click the vertical title to expand it again. The adjacent arrow buttons do the same.</p><p>In the bottom dock, click the selected Issues, Probe table, Power tree, Console or Notifications title to hide that view. Click any title to show it.</p><p>Use the minus or minimize button on Board Nets, PI setup or the EM result manager to move it into the bottom tool shelf. Double-click another floating panel's title to minimize it there. Click its bottom restore button to bring it back with its current settings. Minimized dialogs also hide their backdrop.</p><p>Open detached assembly, report, result, probe and trace windows have bottom controls to bring them forward, including after OS minimization. If restoring fails, the control remains available for retry and a coded status message explains recovery.</p><p>Drag a heading to move or redock a panel. Dividers resize panels, and pinning controls whether a side panel auto-hides when the pointer leaves.</p></> },
    { id: "picture-guide", title: "Follow the pictures: inspect a real board", keywords: "pictures screenshot visual tutorial Marble layer net name report steps", body: <>
      <p className="help-lead">Use the pinned Marble board to learn what the interface is showing before interpreting a result.</p>
      <p>These are unchanged SPIKE 0.2.12 browser captures from 20 September 2026, not native-worker runs. Select a picture to enlarge it. The pictured ribbon is older: Home / PI / HF-SI / EMI / Thermal. The current workspace puts Mesh and Solve directly after Home. Follow the named workflow controls rather than matching every tab position. Animated guides elsewhere are illustrations, not simulations.</p>
      <h2>1. Identify the board and runtime</h2>
      <Figure src="/help/marble-workspace-3d.png" caption="Actual Marble v1.4.4 browser workspace. Component bodies are procedural placeholders; no native solve is demonstrated." />
      <Steps><li>Import your board, then confirm the project name and physical copper-layer count in Scene Navigator.</li><li>Read the runtime indicator: a preview-only browser cannot run the native desktop worker.</li><li>Review Design readiness and Issues. Imported geometry and visible component bodies are not a passed electrical analysis.</li></Steps>
      <h2>2. Separate layers before selecting a terminal</h2>
      <Figure src="/help/marble-layout-layers.png" caption="Actual All-copper view with Layer Manager open. The pictured layer rows and opacity controls affect inspection, not the electrical domain." />
      <Steps><li>Switch to 2D and open Layers. Use the named layer rows to reduce visual overlap.</li><li>Choose the selection filter and inspect the selected object's net, layer and identity before making it a terminal or probe.</li><li>Keep physical copper layers distinct from mask, silk and display-only geometry.</li></Steps>
      <h2>3. Zoom until fine-trace names are readable</h2>
      <Figure src="/help/marble-net-names.png" caption="Actual F.Cu view at 4441% zoom. Text identifies imported nets; no voltage or current field is shown." />
      <Steps><li>Enable View → Show net names, or the Net names control in Layer Manager.</li><li>Select a copper layer and zoom into a trace. Pan to follow the route; return to Fit to restore board context.</li><li>Missing text at a wide zoom is not proof that copper has no net. Confirm the net in the selection inspector.</li></Steps>
      <h2>4. Recognize an unsolved report</h2>
      <Figure src="/help/marble-report-preview.png" caption="Actual report preview: ANALYSIS NOT RUN, no numerical model result attached. Blank values are not zero-valued results." />
      <Steps><li>Open Reports → Engineering. Read the status banner before the tables.</li><li>If analysis has not run, keep the report as a setup record. Define exact terminals, review a supported solver, preview the mesh and run the analysis before interpreting numerical output.</li><li>For a returned result, preserve model status, warnings, units and convergence evidence alongside the report.</li></Steps>
      <p>Continue with <TopicLink id="pi-setup">PI setup</TopicLink>, the <TopicLink id="recorded-pic-dc">recorded DC interpretation example</TopicLink>, or the <TopicLink id="feature-coverage">feature coverage map</TopicLink>.</p>
      <h2>Source identity</h2><p>BerkeleyLab/Marble v1.4.4, commit <code>a426777d92c0f22a546d4740b419a3937e0c1f90</code>. Existing image hashes, CERN OHL v1.2 attribution and capture limitations remain in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and Integrated help maintenance and coverage.</p>
    </> },
    { id: "recorded-pic-dc", title: "Read a recorded DC result", keywords: "real simulation example PIC voltage drop copper loss completed approximate warnings", body: <>
      <p>This is a numerical interpretation example from a separate local PI-only evaluation on 23 September 2026. It is not a screenshot or qualification of the current full workbench.</p>
      <h2>What was recorded</h2><p>The PIC programmer example used net <code>/pic_sockets/VCC_PIC</code>, a 1 V source and a 0.1 A load with an implicit return. These are demonstration boundary conditions, not recommended board operating values.</p>
      <div className="help-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Recorded field</th><th>Value</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Operation / model</td><td>completed / approximate</td><td>Completion did not establish convergence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum voltage drop</td><td>16.382762821 mV</td><td>About 1.6383% of the stated 1 V source.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total copper loss</td><td>1.638276282 mW</td><td>For this load and modeled conductor network.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mesh</td><td>767 nodes / 787 edges</td><td>A discretization record, not an accuracy rating.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <h2>Read the warnings with the values</h2><Steps><li>Keep the approximate status: the artifact requests a convergence run before sign-off.</li><li>Inspect the 49 excluded branches outside the driven component before deciding the analyzed network is complete.</li><li>Review the rounded-rectangle pad approximation and absent package/contact resistance.</li><li>Do not interpret the low linear residual as proof of correct geometry, terminals or physical accuracy.</li></Steps>
      <h2>Reproducibility record</h2><p>Analysis ID <code>ten-board-pic-programmer-dc</code>; evidence contract <code>spike/pi-report-evidence/v1</code>; generated UTC <code>2026-09-23T21:06:18.944Z</code>. The reviewed file is <code>pic-real-dc-evidence.json</code>, SHA-256 <code>d87bdd05b901075d7fea91d6a56a431c1c6de8f98310220a53a674688d66ac8b</code>.</p>
      <p>Source: KiCad source mirror revision <code>ee0fde406f1e2f8a39ac6a58ec062d8128bf202a</code>, <code>demos/pic_programmer/pic_programmer.kicad_pcb</code>; board SHA-256 <code>1ca70b620506818eb0227e2863bbd573d1fec1672072dc43d0dbe8475e69c8fb</code>. Raw board/result artifacts are not bundled with this guide. No new schematic or result screenshot is reproduced.</p>
      <p>Use <TopicLink id="results">Results and probes</TopicLink> for the workflow and <TopicLink id="feature-coverage">coverage and missing evidence</TopicLink> before treating any other feature as demonstrated.</p>
    </> },
    { id: "feature-coverage", title: "Feature coverage and missing evidence", keywords: "all features coverage gaps real screenshots illustrated simulation tested controls matrix", body: <>
      <p>Find instructions by feature family. “Pictured” describes shipped evidence, not exhaustive testing. The control catalog lists source locations; it does not prove every runtime state works. Animated guides explain concepts and do not contain solver output.</p>
      <div className="help-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature / instructions</th><th>Available picture or example</th><th>Still missing</th></tr></thead><tbody>{helpFeatureCoverage.map(row => <tr key={row.family}><td><TopicLink id={row.topic}>{row.family}</TopicLink></td><td>{row.pictured}</td><td>{row.gap}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p><TopicLink id="picture-guide">Follow the Marble pictures</TopicLink> or open <TopicLink id="evidence">Screenshots and recorded results</TopicLink> for original capture scope. Reproducible current desktop captures remain needed across the gaps above.</p>
    </> },
    { id: "quick-start", title: "Quick start", keywords: "new open import save run results Marble", body: <><p>Use this sequence for a first DC PI review. Save the project before running long operations so the embedded design snapshot, terminals, mesh settings, and results have a reproducible package.</p><Figure src="/help/marble-workspace-3d.png" caption="Marble v1.4.4 source-imported in the SPIKE 0.2.12 local browser workspace. Component bodies are procedural models; this does not demonstrate the native desktop worker. This historical capture has the older HF/SI, EMI and Thermal ribbon; the current workspace has dedicated Mesh and Solve tabs." /><Steps><li>Choose <b>File → New project</b> or <b>Open project</b>.</li><li>Import a supported board snapshot and inspect the import-quality report.</li><li>Open <b>PI → DC drop</b>, select the power net, then place source, load, and return terminals on exact copper.</li><li>Run <b>Preview mesh</b> and resolve every blocking preflight issue.</li><li>Run DC, inspect fields and probes, then choose <b>Reports → Engineering</b>.</li></Steps><DemoFrame caption="The full first-analysis loop: import, place terminals, preview the mesh, solve, then report. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><WorkspaceTourDemo /></DemoFrame><Figure src="/help/marble-report-preview.png" caption="Marble report preview with ANALYSIS NOT RUN. It documents setup only and is not numerical-result evidence." /><p>Verify the displayed project name, result identity, model status and warnings before export.</p></> },
    { id: "saved-results", title: "Save and load simulations", keywords: "save load results waveform mesh ODB++ model layer portable history copy result file", body: <><p><b>Save</b> retains the editable project, including its simulation records, setup, fields, meshes, waveforms, probes, and display settings. Imported 3D board/part scenes and 2D layer files are embedded in the <code>.spike</code> package, so reopening can restore them without the original model folders or rerunning CAD export. A save waits for an active visual import to finish.</p><Steps><li>Run an analysis and inspect its result status and warnings.</li><li>Choose <b>Save</b> or <b>Save As</b> for the complete editable project. Choose <b>Save project copy without results</b> when you need a separate package containing the design and setup but no captured result payloads.</li><li>Choose <b>Save results file</b>, <b>PI → Save results</b>, or <b>Reports → Save results</b> to write a standalone <code>.spike-results.json</code> snapshot with its embedded design and viewing context.</li><li>Choose <b>Open project</b> for a <code>.spike</code> project or result file, or <b>Open results file</b> / <b>Load results</b> for a result snapshot. Select the restored run in the result controls to inspect fields, waveforms, probes, or the saved 2D/3D view.</li></Steps><p>ODB++ designs retain canonical manufacturing geometry and source details; the original job archive is not required to reopen the normalized board. Unknown fields from newer project extensions are preserved when saving. Older v1 result exports without an embedded design require their original board to be open first. Use a full <code>.spike</code> project for multi-board or MCAD portability.</p><p>Saving reports an error if a result exceeds the current 96 MiB artifact transport limit or imported visual data exceeds 96 MiB in one save. The existing project file stays intact. Missing source models remain identified as missing; saving does not fabricate them.</p></> },
    { id: "projects", title: "Projects and files", keywords: "spike package save as recent checksum project manager link manager upgrade older format", body: <><p>A <code>.spike</code> v3 file is a versioned ZIP64 project package with a canonical JSON manifest and checksummed design, source, model, result, report, audit, and extension members. It stores the embedded design source, stackup, model assignments, PI/SI topology, terminals, mesh configuration, visualization state, probes, results, EMI setup/preflight/screening, thermal scenario, assembly links, and source records.</p><h2>File commands</h2><ul><li><b>Save</b> stores the complete project with results at the approved path.</li><li><b>Save As</b> creates a new package and updates the active project path.</li><li><b>Save project copy without results</b> writes a separate package with design, setup, and board links; the active project keeps its results.</li><li><b>Save results file</b> writes a standalone <code>.spike-results.json</code> file. Open it directly with <b>Open project</b> or <b>Open results file</b>.</li><li><b>Save instance</b> creates a timestamped independent package.</li><li><b>Project manager</b> reports package identity, board counts, result count, and recent paths.</li></ul><p>Legacy <code>spike-project-package/v1</code> and v2 projects are migrated in memory. After opening, SPIKE offers <b>Upgrade now</b> to save a new v3 package while preserving the older file, or <b>Later</b> to keep working. A later explicit save also emits v3. Signed v3 projects are verified by the native desktop host before their workspace state is applied.</p></> },
    { id: "multi-board-graph", title: "Multiple boards and connector graph", keywords: "multi board import connector graph auto help direct mate virtual harness route", body: <><p>Save the active board as a .spike project. Open MCAD assembly and choose <b>Import boards / external assemblies</b> to select several KiCad, IPC-2581, or .spikeassembly sources in one operation. The import commits all sources together and retains the existing assembly.</p><Steps><li>Open the Link Manager after at least two boards are present. The connector graph discovers J, P and CN references on every retained board.</li><li>Choose two connector ports in the graph or endpoint lists. Review suggested pin pairs; shared net names alone do not establish a physical connection.</li><li>Choose a direct mate or a virtual cable harness. For a harness, preview the routed path and cut length after setting slack, clearance, waypoints and keepouts.</li><li>Add the reviewed link to the draft, then choose <b>Save boards and links</b>. Saved virtual routes appear in the 3D assembly view.</li></Steps><p>Use the explicit connector form when an imported design does not identify a connector. The graph does not qualify mating, bend radius, electrical ratings or coupled PI/SI/thermal/EMI effects.</p></> },
    { id: "navigation", title: "Viewport and selection", keywords: "2d 3d orbit pan zoom center pivot select filter context net names multi board assembly", body: <><p>The viewport supports separate 2D and 3D interaction policies. Use the selection filter to constrain picking to all objects, components, or nets. The inspector shows object identifiers, coordinates, layers, connected nets, footprint metadata, and solved values where available.</p><p>In a multi-board assembly, select the intended board occurrence before PI, thermal, or EMI analysis. The selected occurrence must use the active detailed design. Repeated occurrences of that design need an explicit selection. Board-only results name the selected board and exclude other assembly entities. In MCAD assembly, declare stacked board connector mates with two board::connector identities and a one-to-one pin map; use harness rows for cables. Use the SI multi-board workflow's explicit board jobs when reviewing several signal paths.</p><Figure src="/help/marble-layout-layers.png" caption="Marble in the 2D viewport with All copper selected and the layer manager open." /><Figure src="/help/marble-net-names.png" caption="Marble F.Cu at 4441% zoom, where fine-trace net labels are visible." /><ul><li>3D: left drag orbits, middle/right drag pans, wheel zooms.</li><li>Middle-click without dragging sets the 3D orbit center on the surface under the cursor. The same action is available from the viewport context menu.</li><li>2D: drag pans and wheel zooms; orbit is disabled.</li><li>Choose <b>View → Show net names</b> to switch to 2D and label visible copper, or use <b>Layer manager → Scene → Net names</b>. Fine-trace labels appear as you zoom in. The setting is saved with the project.</li><li>Use the context menu for global selection commands and task-specific terminal, probe, or isolation actions.</li></ul><DemoFrame caption="Middle-click sets a pivot on the board; every later orbit stays centered on it. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><OrbitPivotDemo /></DemoFrame></> },
    { id: "analytics-overview", title: "Analysis workflows and evidence", keywords: "analytics overview PI SI EM thermal hybrid multisolve CHT CFD multiboard prerequisites inputs outputs diagnostics conservation convergence", body: <><p>Choose the question first, then the narrowest workflow that returns the required quantity. Each workflow below separates setup, preparation and execution. A prepared case or visible plot is not a completed result.</p><ul><li><TopicLink id="analytics-pi">PI analytics</TopicLink>: voltage, drop, current, density, impedance and transient electrical behavior.</li><li><TopicLink id="analytics-si">SI analytics</TopicLink>: sampled networks, channels, crosstalk, eye and TDR outputs.</li><li><TopicLink id="analytics-em">EM analytics</TopicLink>: screening, port sweeps and admitted spatial or far-field results.</li><li><TopicLink id="analytics-thermal">Thermal analytics</TopicLink>: object nodes, board grids and retained solid volumes.</li><li><TopicLink id="analytics-hybrid">Hybrid and multi-solve</TopicLink>: explicit coupling and independent-job scheduling.</li><li><TopicLink id="analytics-cht">Conjugate heat transfer</TopicLink> and <TopicLink id="analytics-cfd">CFD and airflow</TopicLink>: bounded reference and adapter routes.</li><li><TopicLink id="analytics-multiboard">Multi-board analytics</TopicLink>: reduced coupled studies and retained-volume electrothermal work.</li></ul><p>For every result record the design/revision, solver and version, model status, units, assumptions, residuals, convergence comparison and warnings. Use the <TopicLink id="validation">validation guide</TopicLink>, <TopicLink id="doc:docs/ANALYSIS_GALLERY.md">analysis gallery</TopicLink> and <TopicLink id="doc:docs/UI_QUICKSTART.md">current UI quick start</TopicLink>. Workflow diagrams explain sequence; browser screenshots show observed interface state; executed plots show solver output only when their fixture, inputs and result status are recorded. None alone establishes physical qualification.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-pi", title: "PI analytics", keywords: "power integrity DC IR drop AC impedance transient voltage current density mesh convergence KCL", body: <><p>Prerequisites are an imported design, physical stackup, selected power and return domain, exact source/load terminals and an available compatible solver. Open <b>PI analysis setup</b> from search or <b>Analyze → Workspaces → PI</b>; use the shared <b>Mesh</b> and <b>Solve</b> workspaces when directed.</p><Steps><li>Choose DC IR Drop, AC Impedance Sweep or Transient PI and enter source/load/contact values in their displayed units.</li><li>Preview the actual mesh and resolve terminal ownership, disconnected copper, memory and time-grid diagnostics.</li><li>Run and inspect status plus voltage/drop, branch current, current density, loss, impedance or waveform outputs actually returned.</li><li>Compare refinement levels. Review KCL, source/load balance, residual and conditioning diagnostics; a completed solve with unstable density or impedance is not converged.</li></Steps><p>The routed-copper DC path is available within its geometry and convergence limits. PEEC, hybrid, EBrake and experimental owned-pad routes retain their stated model status; current-density convergence and AC/passivity gaps remain open for identified fixtures. No PI route automatically models regulators, arbitrary packages or hardware qualification.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-si", title: "SI analytics", keywords: "signal integrity S parameters RLGC Touchstone crosstalk eye TDR passivity reciprocity convergence", body: <><p>Prerequisites are explicit ports/endpoints and returns, a finite increasing frequency grid, impedance and timing assumptions, and either admitted sampled data, supplied RLGC, or a supported bounded geometry extraction. Open <b>HF / SI</b> through Workspaces and use <b>Source-to-receiver workflow</b>, <b>NEXT / FEXT</b> or the relevant extension route.</p><Steps><li>Review port ordering, reference impedance, DC coverage and source/receiver models.</li><li>Run the sampled-network or channel workflow and inspect S-parameters, transfer, crosstalk, waveform, eye or TDR outputs that were actually produced.</li><li>Review passivity, reciprocity, fit error, interface/global power balance and time-grid diagnostics. Refine frequency span and spacing before interpreting time-domain transforms.</li></Steps><p>Supplied uniform, cascade and star RLGC models are experimental. Bounded canonical path extraction does not provide unrestricted PCB field extraction. Eye, TDR and crosstalk outputs do not establish BER or protocol compliance; nonlinear IBIS switching, AMI, vias/launches/bends, geometry-derived thermal deposition and measured qualification remain capability-specific or open.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-em", title: "EM analytics", keywords: "electromagnetic EMI screening openEMS ports fields far field NF2FF mesh convergence radiation compliance", body: <><p>Start in <b>EM</b> with candidate/return nets and traceable excitation data. Screening accepts reviewed PI, transient, SPICE, measured or manual inputs; a field run additionally needs admitted geometry, materials, explicit ports, frequencies, mesh/resource limits and a runnable trusted extension.</p><Steps><li>Run Preflight and Risk screen. Treat screening metrics as prioritization data, not solved fields.</li><li>For openEMS, review conductor-to-conductor ports and the final admitted grid before Run.</li><li>Inspect only returned E/H, S-parameter, Poynting or NF2FF samples. Review energy/power balance, mesh refinement, port/reference identity, run digest and model status.</li></Steps><p>The retained-volume EM route and current openEMS adapter are experimental within their recorded fixtures. Arbitrary PCB and rigid-flex translation, drilled features, production multiport calibration, measured chamber correlation, immunity and compliance verdicts remain unqualified or unsupported. Display contours and angular spheres do not create solved samples.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-thermal", title: "Thermal analytics", keywords: "thermal object node board grid volume temperature heat flow energy balance steady transient convergence", body: <><p>Choose the model resolution deliberately: <b>Thermal setup</b> for explicit object nodes and surface paths, board-grid setup for approximate layer spreading, or the retained assembly volume workflow for admitted solids and contacts. Provide power, material properties, boundaries, initial conditions and heat capacity where required.</p><Steps><li>Run preflight and check every heat source and boundary has an owner and unit.</li><li>Inspect object temperatures, board fields or nodal/cell volume fields according to the selected model. A marker overlay is not a spatial board field.</li><li>Review heat-flow or energy balance, peak histories, residuals, nonlinear iteration and spatial/time refinement. Prescribed resistances and virtual sinks remain assumptions.</li></Steps><p>Object nodes and board grids are approximate; the board grid uses sampled effective copper and simplified depth cells. Retained solid conduction and matched contacts are experimental. Package gradients, automatically derived cooling paths, general airflow, measured calibration and production qualification require separate evidence.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-hybrid", title: "Hybrid coupling and multi-solve", keywords: "hybrid multisolve multi solve electrothermal coupled circuit field parallel worker batch conservation convergence", body: <><p>Hybrid studies require explicit ownership at each handoff: electrical loss to a named thermal node or retained volume, temperature back to a declared resistance/material law, and compatible geometry/revision identity. Review the coupling interval, RMS/peak convention, relaxation, iteration limit and stopping tolerances before running.</p><p>Inspect per-domain residuals and a shared energy ledger. Convergence means the declared electrical, thermal and coupling criteria passed; it does not promote either underlying model. Stored energy, transfer power and deposited heat must not be counted twice.</p><p>The <b>Parallel workers</b> surface schedules independent bounded jobs with concurrency and resource limits. It is a multi-solve batch facility, not single-solve MPI and not automatic multiphysics coupling. PALACE/Xyce and extension workbenches are also bounded serial integrations unless their own capability record says otherwise.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-cht", title: "Conjugate heat transfer", keywords: "CHT conjugate heat transfer SU2 OpenFOAM solid fluid interface heat balance convergence", body: <><p>The bundled laminar channel reference solves fully developed plane-channel momentum and solid/fluid energy from a supplied pressure gradient. Its gallery plot shows temperature and axial velocity for that bounded model, not arbitrary enclosure flow. The separate fixed <b>SU2 conjugate heat transfer</b> route is an experimental reference workflow. It needs the declared trusted extension/runtime, its fixed three-zone fluid-and-solid setup, finite boundary data and a passing preflight. Inspect runtime/version hashes, residual histories, interface temperatures, heat rates and global heat-balance mismatch.</p><p>Recorded 16/32-cell stationary and moving reference checks passed their stated gates, while an earlier two-zone moving case failed temperature admission and a 64-cell conduction attempt timed out. The passed cases are not general assembly CHT or mesh independence.</p><p>The retained-assembly airflow compiler can prepare conforming CHT cases and import native fields, but its native execution/acceptance status remains separate. The ordinary Thermal panel's OpenFOAM workflow does not translate enabled object/surface boundaries. Never treat a prepared case, runtime check or compact thermal screen as CHT output.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-cfd", title: "CFD and airflow analytics", keywords: "CFD airflow native cavity SU2 OpenFOAM channel pressure velocity residual turbulence convergence", body: <><p>Use <b>Native CFD cavity benchmark</b> for the bundled experimental 2D laminar square-cavity reference. It returns velocity and pressure fields for the admitted case. Review grid size, residuals and refinement; the recorded native/SU2 Re=100 comparison is isothermal 2D evidence only.</p><p>Use <b>Thermal setup → Optional OpenFOAM CFD and airflow workflow</b> for its separate adapter route: Screen, Preflight, Prepare case, then Run OpenFOAM only when the prepared case is runnable. Interpret only worker-imported fields with their provenance. The compact Screen result is an independent RC estimate, not CFD.</p><p>The internal channel screen uses prescribed fully developed flow and is not a general geometry Navier-Stokes solver. General assembly CAD flow, turbulence, automatic solid-fluid CHT, independent CFD comparison and measured cooling correlation remain open or route-specific.</p></> },
    { id: "analytics-multiboard", title: "Multi-board analytics", keywords: "multiboard multi board assembly PI SI EM thermal connectors contacts retained volume electrothermal conservation", body: <><p>Prerequisites are retained board occurrences, exact occurrence identities, reviewed connector pin maps or harness conductors, saved placement/revision and explicit board, contact and part models. Open <b>Multi-board workspace</b>; use <b>Connector links</b> before a coupled study.</p><Steps><li>Reduced PI/SI share explicit circuits, Thermal uses supplied contact/network properties, and EM uses supplied loop models. Review per-occurrence inputs and returns.</li><li>For retained-volume electrothermal work, map every resistor/contact/device loss to retained tetra volumes and choose the internal thermal kernel or admitted CalculiX route.</li><li>Inspect per-board outputs, connector/contact losses, raw conservation residuals, temperature/resistance iteration and spatial/electrical convergence. Save results with assembly identity; stale placements withhold them.</li></Steps><p>Native multiboard PI DC and SI single-frequency AC volume coupling is steady reduced-circuit electrothermal analysis. It does not infer complete circuits or fields from artwork. General PCB extraction, arbitrary device subcircuits, transient switching, radiation, shielding, airflow and measured multiboard qualification remain open.</p></> },
    { id: "pi-setup", title: "PI analysis setup", keywords: "source sink load return isolated ground pulse transient", body: <><p>Sources and loads may anchor to a pad, via, zone, track coordinate, or connected multilayer copper object. Connected layers are derived from the object and via graph; the visible layer does not constrain the electrical solve.</p><Steps><li>Select a power net or cross-select it in the viewport.</li><li>For transient analysis, set the simulation stop time and integration policy directly below the selected net.</li><li>Pick exact source and load points, or filter the list of pads on the selected net.</li><li>Choose an implicit return, explicit return net, or isolated-secondary domain. See <TopicLink id="pi-returns">Source and load returns</TopicLink> for the electrical boundary and row pairing.</li><li>Preview the mesh and resolve blocking terminal, connectivity, memory, and time-step issues before running.</li></Steps><DemoFrame caption="Source and load anchors snap to exact copper; an explicit dashed return keeps the current loop physical. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><TerminalPlacementDemo /></DemoFrame></> },
    { id: "pi-returns", title: "Source and load returns in PI", keywords: "source return local reference load returns paired current sink ground explicit return isolated secondary 0 V loop drop", body: <>
      <p>In a DC or transient PI solve, the positive source terminal drives the selected supply net and current sinks draw from that net. An explicit return adds the copper path that carries current back to the source. Choose <b>Solve → Review setup → Return path / ground</b>, select <b>Explicit board return / ground</b>, and choose the actual connected return net.</p>
      <h2>Source return / local reference</h2>
      <p>Place the source return on the return copper at the source connector, regulator, or other intended source negative terminal. It is the local <b>0 V reference</b> for the source voltage, not another voltage source or an instruction to connect every ground to an ideal zero-ohm node. The source-positive voltage is measured relative to this anchor. Its contact and package resistance entries apply only when those physical resistances are known and assigned.</p>
      <h2>Load returns</h2>
      <p>Place one load return on the selected return net for each current sink, ideally at that load's ground or negative pin. Rows pair with sinks in the same order: sink 1 with load return 1, sink 2 with load return 2, and so on. The displayed return current follows the sink value automatically; a 10 A sink produces an equal 10 A return flow with the opposite signed injection in the solver request. Review row order after adding or removing sinks. An explicit run is blocked if the number of load returns differs from the number of sinks.</p>
      <h2>What the three loop choices mean</h2>
      <ul><li><b>Rail-only approximation:</b> no physical return copper is solved. Use it to inspect supply-side resistance when the return is intentionally outside the model; do not read its drop as the complete supply loop.</li><li><b>Explicit board return / ground:</b> supply and return copper are both included. Results can separate supply drop, return rise, and end-to-end loop drop.</li><li><b>Isolated transformer secondary:</b> the secondary uses its own local 0 V reference and remains galvanically separate from primary ground. This boundary does not model transformer magnetics, leakage, saturation, or interwinding capacitance.</li></ul>
      <p>Use <b>Pick exact point</b>, a pad from <b>Pads / details</b>, or a selected copper object to anchor each terminal on the named net. A coordinate-only placement can be used for exploration but has weaker terminal-mapping evidence. Preview the mesh and check connected copper, net identity, current balance, and convergence before interpreting the loop result. Completion alone does not upgrade an approximate model to validated.</p>
    </> },
    { id: "transient-pi", title: "Transient PI waveforms", keywords: "step pulse pwl delay rise fall on time period timestep decimation spice", body: <><p>Each source and sink has a visual excitation editor. Choose DC, Step, Pulse, or PWL and inspect the waveform preview before solving. Time inputs accept SPICE engineering notation such as <code>100ns</code>, <code>10us</code>, <code>2ms</code>, and <code>1s</code>; <code>m</code> means milli and mega is written <code>meg</code>.</p><h2>Pulse parameters</h2><ul><li><b>TD</b>: delay before the edge.</li><li><b>TR</b>: rise time from initial/low to final/high value.</li><li><b>PW</b>: high or on-time.</li><li><b>TF</b>: fall time.</li><li><b>PER</b>: repetition period, which must contain TR + PW + TF.</li></ul><DemoFrame caption="Pulse anatomy: delay, rise, on-time, fall, and the period constraint checked before solving. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><PulseAnatomyDemo /></DemoFrame><p>The run controls set stop time, automatic or manual integration step, saved-frame decimation, playback rate, maximum solver time, result-memory budget, capacitance approximation, and initial state. Automatic integration uses the fastest configured edge and sends the recommended step to the worker; frame decimation reduces stored playback data without skipping internal solve steps.</p><p>The native transient engine currently solves geometry-derived resistance and partial-inductance coupling with an optional bounded stackup shunt-capacitance approximation. Nonlinear MOSFETs, regulators, control loops, saturation, and vendor IC models require an explicit ngspice circuit co-simulation and are not inferred from these controls.</p></> },
    { id: "power-tree", title: "Power Tree wiring and SPICE", keywords: "ptree power architecture connect wire rail budget resistor capacitor inductor mosfet ic schematic ngspice model assistant pins", body: <><p>The Power Tree is an editable, component-level source-to-load circuit architecture linked to board nets, pads, and references. It can be extracted from the board or a schematic/netlist, or built manually with electrical symbols. It supports multiple sources, conversion stages, ordered net segments, series components, shunt elements, isolated returns, connectors, harnesses, and multiple loads.</p><h2>Generate a schematic</h2><p>Choose <b>Generate from inputs</b> in the power or signal workbench. Enter a source, net, and destinations, or choose board endpoints. Power inputs also include source voltage and current per load. Preview the schematic, then add it to the sheet or replace the sheet. Board and netlist extraction also opens a reviewable preview. Signal board generation currently supports endpoints on the same net; add multi-net stages explicitly.</p><p>Arrange lays out deep paths from left to right. Pin endpoints align with the orthogonal wires. Use Properties (or double-click a symbol) to edit values and remove individual connections. Undo and Redo are available in the toolbar; each drag is one undo step. Narrow windows start with properties collapsed.</p><p>The thermal setup includes a network generated from entered sources, links, and ambient resistances. The EMI setup includes a test schematic generated from excitation, candidate nets, returns, and requested analyses. Expand either sheet to inspect it. EMI symbols open their corresponding setup section.</p><h2>Build and navigate</h2><Steps><li>Choose <b>Extract from board</b>, <b>Import schematic / netlist</b>, or add a symbol from the left palette.</li><li>Drag blocks to place them. Wheel zooms; middle-drag or Alt-drag pans. Drag empty canvas to marquee-select. Ctrl-click adds or removes a block from the selection.</li><li>Right-click the canvas or a block for select, duplicate, delete, connect, arrange, fit, reset, clear, and help commands. Reset restores the sheet state present when the workbench opened; Clear removes all blocks and wires after confirmation.</li></Steps><h2>Connect blocks</h2><Steps><li>Click the upstream block's labeled <b>OUT</b> port. A dashed wire follows the pointer.</li><li>Click the downstream block's labeled <b>IN</b> port to complete the directed connection. Press Escape or choose <b>Cancel wire</b> to stop without changing the topology.</li><li>For keyboard or batch editing, select the upstream block, Ctrl-click the downstream block, and choose <b>Connect selected</b>. The toolbar and block context menu can also start or finish a wire.</li></Steps><DemoFrame caption="Directed wiring: click the OUT port, then the IN port; Escape cancels without changing topology. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><WireConnectDemo /></DemoFrame><h2>Assign circuit and SPICE behavior</h2><Steps><li>Select a block and choose its <b>Simulation model</b>: R, L, C, diode, MOSFET, source, load, isolated transformer, SPICE subcircuit, or behavioral block.</li><li>Open <b>Model assistant</b>. Enter the primitive and parasitic values or a reviewed model link. For a component model, map each participating pad to input, output, return, control, passive, or unused.</li><li>Use a primitive R/L/C model for a directly composed linear element. Use a model link for reviewed transistor, diode, IC, or subcircuit behavior. SPIKE does not infer nonlinear device behavior from a footprint or value string.</li><li>Choose <b>ngspice</b> for an explicit circuit, or <b>Geometry parasitics + ngspice</b> for staged co-simulation. The first stage extracts board interconnect parasitics. The second stage composes the reviewed circuit and runs it in the isolated ngspice worker.</li></Steps><h2>Validate and hand off</h2><Steps><li>Assign source voltage and load current or power in the active operating case. Add cases for load corners or derating.</li><li>Choose <b>Use for analysis</b>. SPIKE checks topology reachability, source/load definitions, component pin mappings, model readiness, and produces the PI rail jobs.</li><li>Open the SPICE Model Assistant from the PI ribbon to inspect the generated netlist, analysis command, vector bindings, diagnostics, and solver details before running.</li></Steps><p>Unsupported models remain visibly blocked. A valid Power Tree describes intended connectivity; it does not by itself validate a vendor model, extracted parasitic network, or numerical result.</p></> },
    { id: "mesh", title: "Mesh and preflight", keywords: "2.5d 3d mesh preview convergence zones vias", body: <><p>Mesh preview is an in-workspace inspection layer. It must stay clipped to the normalized conductor geometry. A passing preflight confirms that terminals map to connected copper and the configured worker can accept the request; it does not prove numerical convergence.</p><ul><li>Use 2.5D connected-conductor mesh for current DC and quasi-static PEEC paths.</li><li>Use 3D conductor-volume preview for geometry inspection and solver-plugin exchange. It is not a full-wave result by itself.</li><li>Run the convergence study and compare at least three mesh sizes for publishable DC work.</li></ul><DemoFrame caption="Coarse, nominal, and fine densities: a convergence study compares results across all three. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><MeshRefineDemo /></DemoFrame></> },
    { id: "plot-interactions", title: "Plot axes, navigation and clipboard", keywords: "graph plot axes grid crosshair logarithmic zoom pan copy image data clipboard paste traces", body: <><p>Use <b>Axes</b> for linear/log scales, automatic or fixed bounds in displayed units, major/minor grids and hover crosshairs. Non-positive coordinates prevent switching to log. Axis settings change the view only.</p><p>Ordinary wheel movement scrolls the containing panel. Ctrl+wheel zooms around the pointer; Shift+wheel pans horizontally. Select Pan or Box zoom before dragging; use Orbit or Pan 3D in a spatial plot. Fit restores the whole plot. Focus the graph for arrow-key pan, +/− zoom and 0 fit.</p><p>Right-click or Shift+F10 opens plot actions. Copy image (Ctrl+C) places a PNG on the clipboard; Download PNG is a fallback. Copy data (Ctrl+Shift+C) produces spreadsheet-ready Series/X/Y samples; spatial/field data uses JSON. Paste traces (Ctrl+V) adds dashed comparisons to a compatible 2D chart. Use Paste X/Y table for manual entry.</p><p>Clipboard comparisons never become solver results or saved project data. Units must match when declared; no conversion is performed. Blank samples remain gaps. Clear pasted removes comparisons; changing the result view also clears them. Native clipboard support depends on the WebView.</p></> },
    { id: "results", title: "Results and probes", keywords: "voltage drop current density power impedance overlay contour probes", body: <><p>Results are explicit datasets: absolute voltage, relative drop, current, current density, power loss, impedance, mesh, and vector fields. A disabled mode means the selected solver did not produce that quantity.</p><ul><li>Use board opacity and translucent mode to preserve spatial context.</li><li>Toggle vectors independently from scalar heatmaps.</li><li>Universal probes report every available quantity at the mapped mesh location; V, I, P, and Z probes narrow the display.</li><li>Overview and per-layer views share result filters and camera state.</li></ul><DemoFrame caption="A universal probe reads every available quantity where you click; V/I/P/Z probes narrow the readout. Illustrative guide, not a recorded simulation."><ProbeDemo /></DemoFrame></> },
    { id: "reports", title: "Reports", keywords: "preview print html export evidence analytics", body: <><p>Report preview is generated inside SPIKE before export. The report includes executive summary, analysis definition, source/load table, stackup, numerical analytics, probe table, field graphs, warnings, solver details, result status, and saved run settings.</p><Figure src="/help/marble-report-preview.png" caption="Marble report preview with ANALYSIS NOT RUN. It is an unsolved setup record, not numerical-result evidence." /><p>Confirm that preview identity names the active project and selected result before treating its tables or plots as evidence.</p><p>Use <b>Print</b> for the operating-system print or PDF workflow. Use <b>Export HTML</b> for the interactive self-contained report.</p></> },
    { id: "spice", title: "Circuit simulation in SPIKE", keywords: "ngspice SPIKES backend circuit netlist transient component stress", body: <><p>The circuit workspace offers a built-in linear solver, an optional SPIKES backend for supported structured circuits, and an ngspice path for explicit circuit models. Choose the engine in the Run tab. SPIKES Studio is a separate application and is not included in SPIKE.</p><p>Review the composed netlist, model assignments, pin mapping, and available engine before running. The ngspice path uses an isolated process and rejects includes, libraries, shell commands, and control blocks. SPIKES backend results stay circuit data and are not projected onto the board.</p><p>For component stress analysis, bind voltage, current, and power vectors to component IDs and ratings. The result records its engine and execution limits.</p></> },
    { id: "error-codes", title: "Errors and troubleshooting", keywords: "error warning performance critical security code frontend backend console license package signature trust", body: <><p>Every process-boundary diagnostic uses <code>SPIKE-ORIGIN-DOMAIN-CLASS-NNNN</code>. <code>FE</code> identifies the interface/native-host tier and <code>BE</code> identifies the worker or solver tier. Record the complete code and operation ID before changing the setup.</p><h2>Class identifiers</h2><ul><li><code>I</code> information</li><li><code>W</code> engineering warning requiring review</li><li><code>P</code> performance or resource budget</li><li><code>E</code> recoverable or terminal operation error</li><li><code>C</code> unexpected critical failure</li><li><code>S</code> security or trust-policy rejection</li></ul><h2>Native .spike package trust</h2><ul><li><code>SPIKE-BE-PACKAGE-S-0001</code>: the package signature contract or algorithm is unsupported. Obtain a package signed with a supported SPIKE release contract.</li><li><code>SPIKE-BE-PACKAGE-S-0002</code>: the signed manifest payload or declared digest is malformed or does not match. Re-export or obtain an unmodified package.</li><li><code>SPIKE-BE-PACKAGE-S-0003</code>: the package signature is malformed or fails verification. Do not open the package; obtain a new copy from its trusted source.</li><li><code>SPIKE-BE-PACKAGE-S-0004</code>: the signing key is absent from this build's pinned trust registry or the registry is unavailable. Install the correct SPIKE build or ask the package publisher to use an approved signing key.</li></ul><p>Unsigned packages remain explicitly identified as unsigned. Do not add an untrusted key or bypass a signature failure to recover a project.</p><h2>Recovery sequence</h2><Steps><li>Save the project to a new path if the interface remains responsive.</li><li>Copy the exact code, operation ID, product/worker version, and bounded Console detail.</li><li>Correct the condition named by Issues or the error catalog. Retry only after the input, runtime, or resource condition changes.</li><li>For a repeated critical or security failure, stop retrying and preserve a sanitized minimal fixture.</li></Steps><h2>Common groups</h2><ul><li><code>FE/BE-IPC</code>: restart the matched desktop and worker; do not mix build versions.</li><li><code>BE-IMPORT</code>: review unsupported or malformed source entities and the Import Report.</li><li><code>BE-MESH</code>: correct disconnected terminals/geometry or revise an approved resource budget.</li><li><code>BE-SOLVER</code>: inspect conditioning, convergence, validity range, and solver details.</li></ul><p>The complete canonical catalog is installed with the documentation at <code>docs/ERROR_CODE_CATALOG.md</code>; task-oriented recovery procedures are in <code>TROUBLESHOOTING.md</code>. Numerical validity remains separate: a software operation can finish while its result remains approximate, unsupported, or failed to converge.</p></> },
    { id: "emi", title: "EM test workbench", keywords: "em electromagnetics emi emc openems radiation screening ports near field far field compliance", body: <><p>The EM tab has its own staged setup and review flow. It preserves candidate and return nets, pre-pass metrics, explicit ports, frequency, environment, mesh, solver limits, readiness issues, EMI screening, and structured field results in the project.</p><Steps><li>Choose <b>Net domain</b> and select candidate and return nets.</li><li>Provide traceable PI, transient, SPICE, measured, or reviewed manual values for dV/dt, dI/dt, peak current, loop area, and return discontinuities.</li><li>Run <b>Preflight</b>, then <b>Risk screen</b> to prioritize engineering review.</li><li>For a full-wave case, select <b>far field</b>, define explicit conductor-to-conductor ports, and review the domain mesh before preparing openEMS input.</li><li>Run the prepared case and open <b>Far field</b> to inspect frequency-selectable NF2FF polar cuts, radiated power, directivity, sample counts, and validity requirements.</li></Steps><p>EMI screening alone does not calculate fields or compliance. A completed openEMS NF2FF result is numerical field data, but remains unvalidated for that PCB until mesh convergence and independent trusted-solver or measured correlation are attached. Near-field and standards-compliance verdicts remain capability-gated.</p></> },
    { id: "solver-manager", title: "Solver manager", keywords: "external engine openEMS OpenFOAM ngspice tuning", body: <><p>Open <b>Settings - External engines - Solver manager</b> to see the solvers used by desktop workflows and adjust supported settings. The engine list shows openEMS, ngspice, and OpenFOAM; their analysis setup remains in the relevant EM, SPICE, or Thermal workspace.</p><ul><li><b>Register</b> records a local runtime path. It does not install or run the engine.</li><li><b>Forget</b> removes the registration without deleting local files.</li><li>Solver selection does not substitute another engine if the selected one is unavailable.</li></ul></> },
    { id: "importing", title: "Importing designs", keywords: "kicad pcb import quality report zones pads vias stackup ipc odb cad neutral", body: <><p>SPIKE ingests a board snapshot into a source-independent design model. Native KiCad ingestion is the first-class path; IPC-2581 and ODB++ coverage is growing behind the same CAD-neutral importer contract, so workflows never see parser classes or vendor objects.</p><h2>Import workflow</h2><Steps><li>Import the board file from the Home or Project surfaces. Large boards show progress while copper, stackup, footprints, nets, and 3D model assignments are extracted.</li><li>Open the import-quality report in the Scene Navigator. It reports per-category coverage (tracks, pads, vias, zones, components, drawings) plus unsupported or malformed entities by identifier.</li><li>Check the layer stack: physical layer order, copper thickness, dielectric material, and rigid/flex regions drive every downstream solve.</li><li>Cross-check net inventory against your schematic before placing terminals; the importer preserves source UUIDs so selections round-trip to the EDA tool where supported.</li></Steps><h2>Known fidelity boundaries</h2><ul><li>Zone fills are meshed from their extracted outline and fill geometry; custom pad polygons, castellations, plated slots, and antipad semantics have explicit support states shown in the report.</li><li>3D component models decorate the scene only; they never contribute to electrical results unless a thermal or field analysis explicitly consumes assigned models.</li><li>Malformed archives fail closed with a named import error code instead of a partial silent load.</li></ul></> },
    { id: "volume-engines", title: "CalculiX and Gmsh volume studies", keywords: "thermal CalculiX Gmsh mesh multiboard enclosure radiation convection conduction external solver", body: <><p>Solver and mesher preferences are independent in the analysis setup dock. CalculiX supplies experimental steady solid thermal analysis. Gmsh supplies complete tetrahedral geometry; its PI, SI and EM routes require an explicit solver translator.</p><Steps><li>Save the assembly project with retained board occurrences and admitted mechanical solids.</li><li>Choose Gmsh under Mesher and open Mesher setup. Assign reviewed effective layer and solid materials, set mesh limits and generate the volumes.</li><li>Assign heat sources, prescribed temperature/flux or ambient convection/radiation, and explicit matched contacts.</li><li>Choose CalculiX under Solver and open Solver setup. Prepare and review the case, then run the separately installed engine.</li><li>Inspect actual temperatures and save with results. Embedded results reopen directly; changed geometry or boundary data requires a new solve.</li></Steps><p>Missing runtimes, stale bindings, unsupported geometry and resource limits block execution. Visual component models are not automatically physical solids. Omitted exterior boundaries are adiabatic. This workflow does not solve airflow or cavity radiosity and is not production qualified.</p></> },
    { id: "thermal", title: "Thermal analysis", keywords: "thermal steady transient BOM ODB++ conduction convection radiation surface object heat capacity SPIKE OpenFOAM", body: <><p>The Thermal tab runs SPIKE’s built-in steady or transient lumped object thermal solver without OpenFOAM. It reports approximate object temperatures and per-boundary steady heat flows, not a spatial PCB temperature field.</p><Steps><li>Open <b>Thermal → Thermal setup</b>. Sync board parts or import BOM CSV/TSV and review reference, power W, top Rth K/W and bottom Rth K/W mappings. ODB++ named component properties can also populate those values.</li><li>Under <b>Object and surface heat transfer</b>, select any enabled object, a whole object, X/Y/Z face, or named CAD surface. Add explicit conduction K/W to ambient or another object, convection h W/m²K with exposed area mm², or radiation emissivity and exposed area. Optional sink or surroundings temperatures override ambient.</li><li>Review the actual exposed area and avoid repeating a top/bottom path. For transient runs, give every included object heat capacity J/K, duration and time step.</li><li>Run the SPIKE solver. Inspect object temperatures, transient samples, steady surface heat flows, diagnostics, then save the scenario or engineering report.</li></Steps><h2>Validity boundary</h2><ul><li>Each object has one temperature node; a face label does not resolve a temperature gradient.</li><li>Geometry, materials, contacts and fans do not automatically create a heat path. The radiation model uses fixed surroundings, without view factors.</li><li>Optional OpenFOAM CFD does not translate explicit object/surface boundaries; its actions are disabled while any are enabled.</li></ul></> },
    { id: "si-workbench", title: "Signal integrity workbench", keywords: "si s parameters emerge touchstone rlgc eye tdr crosstalk ibis capacitor X7R X5R Y5V resistor thin thick film", body: <><p>In <b>HF / SI</b>, leave the S-parameter solver at <b>SPIKE internal</b>, then open <b>S-parameters → Source-to-receiver workflow</b> to compose an experimental loaded channel study.</p><Steps><li>Generate a uniform RLGC line, import Touchstone, or extract a bounded canonical board path with an explicit reference.</li><li>Define source and receiver ports, impedance, capacitance, package parameters, edge times and thresholds. Additional sources act as deterministic aggressors.</li><li>Attach resistor or capacitor models with grade, tolerance, temperature, parasitics and noise assumptions. All controls have editable defaults.</li><li>Inspect IBIS models, pins, package data, corners and I/V tables. Optional endpoint binding uses an explicitly limited DC-slope/ramp approximation.</li><li>Renormalize, reorder, add port delays, or cascade two-port networks, then run and inspect loaded transfer, crosstalk, receiver waveform and eye, channel TDR, and noise estimates.</li><li>Save setup/results and export RI/MA/DB Touchstone. The channel export excludes endpoint loading; completed results are retained in the project and engineering report.</li></Steps><p>For an imported two-layer board, <b>Check EMerge</b> probes the trusted optional runtime. When it reports <code>si_s_parameters</code>, select EMerge in the S-parameter solver menu. <b>S-parameters</b> and <b>Ports</b> then open its explicit pad-port and frequency-sweep setup. Run the two-port sweep and inspect its plots and unvalidated model status. The floating guide under Help highlights each step.</p><p>For board crosstalk, choose <b>HF / SI → NEXT / FEXT</b>, select an aggressor and a separate victim net, then run the bounded geometry channel. Impedance, NEXT/FEXT, eye, PAM4, protocol suites, and Touchstone import use their existing SI tools. The SI tab, Mesh and Solve workspaces show SI controls and result status when signal integrity is selected.</p><p>The second workbench tab retains geometry/protocol and NRZ/PAM4 tools. Time-domain work requires explicit DC and suitable uniform spacing. Nonlinear IBIS switching, AMI, arbitrary full-wave PCB extraction and protocol compliance remain unavailable. Grade presets are assumptions, not manufacturer guarantees; noise is reported separately from deterministic eye traces.</p></> },
    { id: "validation", title: "Accuracy and validation", keywords: "benchmark standard convergence validated approximate unsupported", body: <><p>Open <b>Help → Accuracy and validation</b> to run the installed corpus. It checks analytical copper resistance, plated-via resistance, zone convergence, hybrid connectivity, native PEEC smoke behavior, a capacitance asymptote, and AC-loss monotonicity.</p><p>These checks are necessary but not sufficient. Independent trusted-solver correlation and measured fixtures remain open gates. Until those gates pass for a solver mode and applicability range, SPIKE reports the result as approximate or solver-dependent rather than globally validated.</p></> },
    { id: "data-tables", title: "Find and edit table rows", keywords: "table grid rows find search compact density page paging keyboard enter shift alt arrow tab", body: <><p>Alternating row bands separate records; the header band contains column titles. Underlined fields with pencils are editable. Tinted controls with chevrons are dropdowns. Locks and dashed underlines mark read-only or unavailable fields; plain text is display-only. Separate buttons run actions, and red-toned buttons remove records. Table palettes follow Settings &gt; Interface &gt; Theme, including system light/dark preferences and high contrast.</p><p>Use <b>Find rows</b> to match all entered terms against displayed values and selected option labels. Use quotes, such as <code>"Source 1"</code>, to find an exact phrase within a cell. <b>Compact</b> changes this table's row density, and page controls appear after 100 row groups. Search keeps the focused row visible until focus leaves the table.</p><ul><li>Tab and Shift+Tab keep normal browser focus order.</li><li>In a single-line input, Enter and Shift+Enter move to the same editable column in the next or previous row.</li><li>Alt+Arrow moves between editable cells and skips disabled or read-only controls.</li><li>Normal arrows, select Enter, and textarea Enter keep their native behavior.</li></ul><p>The PI terminal editor offers <b>Table</b> and <b>Cards</b> layouts with the same records and placement actions. Identifier fields stage text until Enter or leaving the field; Escape cancels. Blank or duplicate IDs show an inline error without changing the record.</p><p>Table tools do not sort, reorder, bulk-paste, validate, or save engineering records. Review the owning workflow's units, warnings, and save or run action.</p></> },
    { id: "shortcuts", title: "Keyboard shortcuts", keywords: "hotkey save open undo redo fit ribbon panels layers", body: <><p>Open <b>Tools - Shortcut manager</b> or press <kbd>{defaultShortcuts.shortcutWindow}</kbd> to review and reassign configurable commands. Assigning a used key swaps the conflicting binding.</p>{shortcutGroups.map(group => <section key={group.label}><h2>{group.label}</h2><ul>{group.actions.map(action => <li key={action}><kbd>{defaultShortcuts[action]}</kbd> {shortcutLabels[action]}</li>)}</ul></section>)}<h2>Editing and files</h2><ul>{fixedShortcuts.map(item => <li key={item.key}><kbd>{item.key}</kbd> {item.label}</li>)}</ul></> },
    { id: "local-llm-mcp", title: "Local LLM and MCP", keywords: "offline LM Studio Ollama MCP model tool automation studies", body: <><p>Open <b>Settings → LLM / MCP</b> in the desktop app and enable the local bridge. It lets an MCP client select PI, SI, EM, or Thermal workspaces; change 2D/3D view; and organize study cases in the open project.</p><Steps><li>In LM Studio, register the local <code>scripts/spike_mcp.py</code> server in <code>mcp.json</code>. For Ollama, start its local API and use <code>scripts/spike_local_chat.py --provider ollama</code>.</li><li>Ask the model to call <code>spike_gui_status</code> or <code>spike_capabilities</code> first. Select a workspace and open run controls to review the setup.</li><li>Read preflight, assumptions, qualification status, and units before using a numerical result. Save the project to retain study changes.</li></Steps><p>The bridge stays on loopback, is off until enabled, and exposes a fixed command list. See <b>docs/LOCAL_LLM_MCP.md</b> for exact commands and troubleshooting.</p></> },
    { id: "native-runtime", title: "Desktop runtime", keywords: "tauri webview native offline windows linux mac", body: <><p>SPIKE is distributed as a native Tauri executable and runs offline. Tauri uses the operating-system web-rendering component for the React interface, so Windows may show a WebView2 child process under SPIKE in Task Manager. That child is part of the native desktop application, not a hosted website.</p><p>Eliminating the WebView process requires a separate Qt, wxWidgets, or other native-widget renderer. It is an application-shell rewrite and does not change solver accuracy.</p></> },
  ];
}
